encyclopedia :hesh do
  name "Hesh"
  summary "Hesh is the lifting and resealing of Lithren's frozen ground over gas-filled cavities, temporarily exposing routes beneath crusts that can settle without warning."
  kind :phenomenon
  subkind :physical_phenomenon
  status :complete
  topics :danger, :navigation, :"outer-system"
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :surface, :cold, :unstable_route] }
  medium "Frozen rubble, volatile frost and enclosed cavities in Lithren's ruin country"
  nature "Gas released beneath a retaining crust raises it until a crack vents the pocket; cooling deposits frost that may close the crack again"
  operating_limit "Requires confined gas and a heat source sufficient to release more; an open dry chamber does not lift merely because it is cold"
  descriptive_identity signs: "A rim of fresh frost follows a clean crack; a slab stands above its older dust line and settles as fine grains stream outward.", effects: "A raised crust exposes low passages and buried faces until pressure falls or frost closes the outlet.", hazards: "The settling roof can pin a traveler; an opened pocket can throw grit and displace air inside a connected shelter."

  prose <<~PROSE
    Lithren cutters call a breathing patch of frozen ground hesh. Volatile frost in an enclosed cavity takes up heat from deeper rock, sunlight conducted through an exposed surface, or recent excavation equipment. Released gas accumulates beneath a crust that retains it. A broad area needs only a modest pressure difference to raise a loose slab. Once a crack opens, escaping gas removes that support.

    The slab may settle at once or rest on fallen pieces while the flow dwindles. At the colder mouth, fresh frost deposits along the crack and can seal it. Another warming can repeat the movement. The working words hesh roof and hesh mouth distinguish the moving cover from the outlet; neither names an ancient mechanism. Old courts, natural fractures and modern spoil can all develop the same condition.

    An exposed mouth can show an underside untouched by previous excavators. Fine grit streams away from it instead of falling directly downward, and a probe may register gas even where the opening remains too narrow to see. A raised slab's clean edge above an older dust line shows how far it moved. Quiet ground without those signs can still contain a pocket that has not yet opened.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "An Opening With a Closing Time"
    Crews sometimes wait beside a mapped mouth to lower a camera, recover a loose object or inspect a blocked threshold from below. Braces set on sound ground can keep a limited opening usable after pressure falls. A rope tied to the moving slab will follow it down. Heating the mouth can release a large quantity of gas before a person has room to escape.

    A broad roof tends to rise little over a large area, admitting probes through long narrow cracks. A small pocket under stacked plates can throw one plate aside and leave a steep shaft. Neither supplies breathable air. Connecting an excavator's pressure shelter to the cavity can bring cold gas into occupied rooms even when the chamber beyond looks still.

    A mouth that freezes shut conceals the route again. Marking its edges on stable surrounding stone lets another crew find it; the marks do not predict its next opening. Instruments can measure an active pocket's pressure and temperature, but unknown connections beneath the ground limit forecasts of how long it will remain raised.
  PROSE

  cue "Fine grains stream sideways from a white-edged crack while the stone above it stands clear of an older dust line."
  cue "A camera shows a roof settling toward its own reflected lamp as the gas reading falls."
  affordance "An active mouth exposes buried undersides and thresholds; a separately braced opening can admit a probe or a small recovery party."
  variation "Wide covers lift shallowly, opening long slots that suit cameras better than travelers."
  variation "Small pockets beneath loose plates can eject their cover and leave an open shaft with unstable walls."
end
